WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.230
Several key bacteria threaten dairy milking operations.

00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:08.490
They drive udder infections, hurt milk quality, and put cow health at risk.

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:12.250
Mastitis is the most costly disease in dairy farming.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:15.890
It damages udder tissue and reduces milk yield.

00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:18.970
Does fighting bacteria take a lot of energy?

00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:20.440
Yes.

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:25.190
When a milking cow's immune system mounts a full response to harmful bacteria —

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:30.660
in her environment, her feed, or her gut — that fight burns a huge amount of energy,

00:00:30.790 --> 00:00:32.040
especially glucose.

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:36.630
In controlled dairy research modeling a severe bacterial challenge,

00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:40.250
a fully activated immune system can use more than one kilogram —

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:44.210
about 2.2 pounds — of glucose in roughly 12 hours.

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:49.070
Milking cows also need glucose to make lactose, the sugar in milk.

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:52.970
When white blood cells are fighting hard, they take priority.

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:58.030
That glucose thief can pull fuel away from the udder and push up the cow's energy cost

00:00:58.080 --> 00:00:59.470
to simply stay well.

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:05.090
In a major challenge, milk can drop sharply — sometimes by tens of pounds in a day —

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:07.970
because energy is diverted from milk to defense.

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:10.110
Where does the enemy hide?

00:01:10.780 --> 00:01:12.830
First, the gut and the feed.

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:18.870
Mold toxins, called mycotoxins, wild yeasts, and harmful bacteria such as E.

00:01:18.940 --> 00:01:22.550
coli and Salmonella in poor-quality feed can injure the gut lining.

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:27.510
That leaky gut forces the immune system to spend resources defending the bloodstream

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:31.590
from toxins and bacterial products that should have stayed inside the gut.

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:35.030
Second, the environment — bedding and mud.

00:01:35.740 --> 00:01:41.210
Environmental bacteria such as Strep uberis constantly challenge teat ends when the cow lies down.

00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:47.090
Battling environmental mastitis is one of the heaviest energy drains a dairy cow can face.

00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:49.650
Third, the rumen.

00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:53.890
A cow depends on billions of helpful microbes to ferment feed into energy.

00:01:54.760 --> 00:01:58.130
Bad actors or sudden ration changes can upset that balance,

00:01:58.180 --> 00:02:02.010
leaving less energy for milk production and normal body functions.

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:06.030
A high-producing dairy cow runs on a tight energy budget.

00:02:06.820 --> 00:02:10.830
Even a quiet infection can push her to burn her own body fat and muscle,

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:14.150
raising the risk of metabolic trouble such as ketosis.

00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:17.750
Keeping bacterial pressure down around the cow matters.

